1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:04,200
Teacher midterm practice for C plus plus, question 10.

2
00:00:04,820 --> 00:00:07,913
Why is A declared as A of two plus one, by two plus

3
00:00:07,913 --> 00:00:11,175
one, when the loops only run i and j up to two?

4
00:00:12,875 --> 00:00:15,908
Look at the declaration on line two: three by three.

5
00:00:16,527 --> 00:00:21,323
Now the loops: i and j go from one to two, so the code uses index two.

6
00:00:22,923 --> 00:00:26,565
An array of size N has indices zero to N minus one.

7
00:00:26,565 --> 00:00:30,522
To use index two, you need a size of at least three.

8
00:00:30,522 --> 00:00:34,353
Index zero is allocated but never used: this is one based

9
00:00:34,353 --> 00:00:38,309
indexing, which matches the math notation A one one.

10
00:00:39,909 --> 00:00:44,031
So the size is three because index two needs a size of three.

11
00:00:44,031 --> 00:00:46,153
Index zero just goes unused.

12
00:00:46,974 --> 00:00:50,014
The off by one error: declaring A of two by two, then

13
00:00:50,014 --> 00:00:53,203
writing to A of two, two, which is out of bounds.

14
00:00:53,923 --> 00:00:58,061
Exam tip: for any array, write the valid index range next to the

15
00:00:58,061 --> 00:01:02,256
declaration, then check the largest index each loop can reach.
